  "body": "Consider:\n\n``` html\n<body>\n    <section>\n        <h1>Foo</h1>\n        <h2>Bar</h2>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n        <h1>Foo2</h1>\n        <h2>Bar2</h2>\n    </section>\n</body>\n```\n\nAs I read the specs, this should be considered semantically as a page with two top-level sections, respectively entitled Foo and Foo2, and having each a subsection.\n\nThat’s not however how recent Firefox and Chromium interpret it. They show all four headings in the same way.\n\nAs I understand the specs, also, removing the `section` tags should not change the outline. But it does, still according to Firefox and Chromium.\n\nAre the browsers wrong? Should the specification be clarified about those points?\n",
